    "chapter_label": "The night sky: Moon and calendars",
    "subtopic_id": "science/grade_08/ch_11/H006",
    "stem": "Mehak's family uses a purely lunar calendar. Each month starts with a new Moon. Twelve lunar months make about 354 days. Seasons on Earth repeat in about 365 days. A harvest day fixed to one lunar month falls earlier in the seasonal year after a few years.\n\nWhich statements correctly explain why the lunar months do not stay aligned with the same seasons?",
    "item_type": "three_statement",
    "options": {
      "A": "1 and 2 only",
      "B": "1 and 3 only",
      "C": "2 and 3 only",
      "D": "1, 2 and 3"
    },
    "statements": [
      {
        "id": "1",
        "text": "Twelve lunar months total about 354 days, which is shorter than the roughly 365-day seasonal cycle."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "text": "Because the lunar year is shorter, the same lunar month drifts earlier relative to the seasons in later years."
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "text": "A purely lunar calendar keeps each lunar month locked to the same season every year because months are still based on Moon phases."
      }
    ],
